Limit Compute Capacity for Applications
Objectives
Objective
•	Learn how to set resource limits (CPU and memory) for containers.
•	Prevent applications from hogging node resources and affecting other workloads.
•	Understand the difference between requests (for scheduling) and limits (for runtime enforcement).
________________________________________
2. Key Concepts
Requests vs Limits
Aspect	Request	Limit
Purpose	Minimum guaranteed resources for scheduling	Maximum resources the container can consume
Scheduler	Uses request to place pods on eligible nodes	Not used for scheduling
Runtime	Container can use more than request if available	Container is throttled (CPU) or killed (memory) if exceeds limit
________________________________________
Memory Limits
•	Define the maximum memory a container can consume.
•	If a container exceeds its memory limit, the OOM killer terminates it.
•	Kubernetes/OpenShift then restarts the pod, based on the restartPolicy (default Always).
•	Prevents applications with memory leaks from taking down the node.
•	Example:
oc set resources deployment/hello --limits memory=1Gi
Or in YAML:
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 500Mi
  limits:
    cpu: 200m
    memory: 1Gi
•	After a pod is OOMKilled, you can see it in the pod status:
lastState:
  terminated:
    exitCode: 137
    reason: OOMKilled
________________________________________
CPU Limits
•	Define the maximum CPU a container can consume.
•	If the container reaches the limit, it slows down, rather than being killed.
•	Ensures consistent performance across nodes and clusters.
•	Example:
oc set resources deployment/hello --limits cpu=200m
________________________________________
Viewing Requests, Limits, and Actual Usage
•	Node level:
oc describe node master01
•	Shows requests, limits, and total allocated resources for CPU and memory.
•	Pod level:
oc adm top pods -n <project>
•	Shows real-time CPU and memory usage per pod.
Example:
oc adm top pods -n openshift-console
NAME                         CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)
console-6689c8589c-bcpb7     1m           48Mi
downloads-79658645bd-hx649   1m           31Mi
•	Can also visualize metrics in the web console under the Metrics tab of a deployment.
________________________________________
3. Best Practices
1.	Set requests to expected peak usage.
o	Ensures the scheduler places pods correctly.
2.	Set limits to protect nodes.
o	Memory: prevents crashes due to leaks.
o	CPU: ensures fair sharing and predictable performance.
3.	Monitor usage regularly via oc adm top or console metrics.
4.	Adjust requests and limits based on observed usage patterns.
________________________________________
4. Quick Commands Summary
Task	Command
Set memory limit	oc set resources deployment/<name> --limits memory=1Gi
Set CPU limit	oc set resources deployment/<name> --limits cpu=200m
Set both via YAML	Add resources.requests and resources.limits under container
View node resources	oc describe node <node-name>
View pod usage	oc adm top pods -n <project>
View node usage	oc adm top nodes
________________________________________
✅ Summary:
•	Requests: affect scheduling (minimum guaranteed).
•	Limits: enforce runtime caps (protect other applications).
•	Memory over-limit = pod killed, CPU over-limit = pod slowed.
•	Use monitoring and adjust values for efficient cluster utilization.

